<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Ablation study of Prompt. Visualization of the diverse and informative diffusion-based augmented images. Columns one to three show images produced using our prompt generator, while columns four to six present images generated using a simple class method.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05722v1/fig_selection.png" id="Sx3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:314/148;" width="314" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Illustration of our selection module. This module has two sub-processes: cosine similarity filtration and foundation-model driven matching. This module takes the source image, synthesized image, and previous mask as input, then produces high-quality filtered images as output.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Conditional Generator via Diffusion</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop our dataset generator using ControlNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang, Rao, and Agrawala 2023</a>)</cite> by leveraging its self-attention and cross-attention layers.
Specifically, given a text prompt <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P^{{}^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P^{{}^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a condition mask <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the module first encodes the text through a text encoder, generating a text embedding <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{R}^{\Lambda}\times d_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{R}^{\Lambda}\times d_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> represents the text length and <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the embedding dimension. The module then concatenates these features, producing the final latent state <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> after <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denoising steps. Finally, the latent state
<math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is decoded by an image decoder, resulting in a high-quality synthetic image <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> that corresponds to the description but with diverse semantics interpretations. Thus, the augmented image can then be generated as described in Equation <a href="#Sx3.E1" title="In Conditional Generator via Diffusion ‣ Methodology ‣ Diffusion Synthesis: Data Factory with Minimal Human Effort Using VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Here, <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sampled noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}({\mathcal{I}}_{i})=\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{I}_{i},\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{i},n\mid\mathcal{M})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}({\mathcal{I}}_{i})=\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{I}_{i},\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{i},n\mid\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">High-quality Image Selection</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To balance the diversity and quality of synthetic data used for training, we implement a human-free image selection strategy to identify and retain high-quality generated samples. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#Sx3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Multi-way Prompts Generator ‣ Methodology ‣ Diffusion Synthesis: Data Factory with Minimal Human Effort Using VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our High-quality Image Selection (HIS) module consists of two sub-components: cosine similarity filtration and foundation model-driven matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, to retain high fidelity in the synthetic data generation process, we introduce a cosine similarity-based selection approach. Specifically, we compute the cosine similarity between the real sample <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and each augmented image <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Generated images that exhibit a cosine similarity greater than a predefined threshold <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> are retained, as stated in Equation <a href="#Sx3.E2" title="In High-quality Image Selection ‣ Methodology ‣ Diffusion Synthesis: Data Factory with Minimal Human Effort Using VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This method ensures that the selected synthetic images closely resemble the original data, thereby enhancing the overall quality and relevance of the augmented dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}^{\prime}}_{i}=cos(\mathcal{I}_{i},\mathcal{D}({\mathcal{I}}_{i}))&gt;\epsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}^{\prime}}_{i}=cos(\mathcal{I}_{i},\mathcal{D}({\mathcal{I}}_{i}))&gt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we employ a foundation model-driven matching block to ensure alignment between masks and generated images. Mismatches can occur because the generator sometimes fails to preserve fine-grained features, such as the presence of multiple objects, as noted in Dataset Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Specifically, we use a segmenter to mask the synthesized image and obtain a mask prediction <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then compare the alignment between the predicted mask <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original pseudo mask <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Synthetic images with low pixel-level label accuracy (i.e., mean Intersection over Union, mIoU) are discarded if there is a significant discrepancy between the predicted and the original masks. This process further improves the quality of the synthetic dataset <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Controlling Balance between Real and Synthetic Data</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training models on synthetic images often risks overemphasizing spurious attributes and biases due to imperfections inherent in generative models. A common approach to mitigate this issue involves assigning different sampling probabilities to real and synthetic images, addressing the imbalance between these data types, as proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. 2022</a>)</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trabucco et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. In our work, we adopt a similar strategy to balance real and synthetic data, as detailed in Equation <a href="#Sx3.E3" title="In Controlling Balance between Real and Synthetic Data ‣ Methodology ‣ Diffusion Synthesis: Data Factory with Minimal Human Effort Using VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability that a synthetic image occupies the <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l-th" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l-th</annotation></semantics></math> position in the mini-batch of images B.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}_{i,j}\in B_{l})=\alpha" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}_{i,j}\in B_{l})=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}_{i,j}\in\mathcal{R}^{H\times W\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}_{i,j}\in\mathcal{R}^{H\times W\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a synthetic image, where <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the index of a particular image. For each image, we generate <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> augmentations, resulting in a synthetic dataset <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}\}\in\mathcal{R}^{N\times K\times H\times W\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{I}^{{}^{\prime}}\}\in\mathcal{R}^{N\times K\times H\times W\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indices <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled uniformly from the <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> real images and their <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> augmented versions, respectively. Given indices <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, a real image <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the batch B with probability <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, its augmented version <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is added.
Thus, we can obtain a large number of augmented samples with richer visual appearance variation while preserving key semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<figure id="Sx4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="3"></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="5">1-shot (mIoU)</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conference</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training-free</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;">Fold-0</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;">Fold-1</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;">Fold-2</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;">Fold-3</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;">Mean<math id="Sx4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
